+# ADR 0121: AI-Native Authoring Parity and Project-Scoped MCP Lifecycle
+
+Status: Accepted
+Date: 2026-08-16
+Amends: ADR 0015
+Relates to: ADR 0016, ADR 0023, ADR 0035, ADR 0117
+Resolves: `AI_FRIENDLY_AUTHORING_SPEC.md` Open Decision #7
+
+## Context
+
+GameEngine already defines authoring as a shared typed and transactional model
+for the human Editor, AI agents, CLI tools, scripts, and tests. The shared
+`engine-authoring` layer contains stable IDs, schemas, commands, transactions,
+diagnostics, scenes, graphs, prefabs, UI documents, materials, project
+settings, and animation data. The Editor already routes important Scene edits
+through `AuthoringCommand`, and Behavior Tree authoring already proves that CLI
+and MCP adapters can share one GUI-free authoring service.
+
+The current AI-facing surface is nevertheless incomplete. `engine-mcp` exposes
+Behavior Tree tool handlers and the AI Agent Bridge, but it does not expose the
+same general Scene, entity, schema, asset, prefab, UI, graph, material, project
+settings, or animation-set authoring capabilities that the Editor can reach.
+ADR 0015 intentionally deferred the MCP transport and process lifecycle, file
+persistence policy, and transaction identity across multiple MCP calls.
+
+ADR 0035's AI Agent Bridge is a different concern. It provides virtual input
+and frame capture so an external agent can observe and drive a running Editor.
+Using mouse coordinates and screenshots as the primary authoring path would
+reintroduce UI-specific behavior and would not satisfy the shared command and
+query contract.
+
+ADR 0117 also strengthens the project-first desktop lifecycle: one Editor
+process owns one concrete `ProjectRoot` for its lifetime. An MCP implementation
+must not bypass that Editor and mutate the same project files behind an active
+in-memory authoring session.
+
+A project-wide decision is therefore required for the standard AI authoring
+entry point, MCP transport ownership, transaction boundaries, parity scope,
+and the implementation order for completing the missing adapters.
+
+## Decision
+
+### 1. MCP is the standard structured AI authoring interface
+
+For a project that is open in the Editor, MCP is the standard structured
+interface used by AI agents to inspect and change authoring data. CLI remains a
+first-class thin adapter for headless scripting, CI, deterministic automation,
+and environments where an MCP client is not available.
+
+The AI Agent Bridge from ADR 0035 remains available for runtime interaction,
+frame observation, visual validation, and genuinely presentation-only Editor
+operations. It is not an alternative authoring model and MUST NOT be used as
+proof that a semantic Editor capability has AI authoring parity.
+
+The intended paths are:
+
+```text
+Human -> Editor UI -> shared authoring service -> command/query -> transaction
+AI    -> MCP       -> shared authoring service -> command/query -> transaction
+Script-> CLI       -> shared authoring service -> command/query -> transaction
+```
+
+MCP MUST NOT invoke CLI commands, parse CLI stdout, or introduce MCP-specific
+editing rules.
+
+### 2. The active Editor owns the initial project-scoped MCP lifecycle
+
+The initial write-capable MCP lifecycle is Editor-attached and project-scoped.
+Each normal Editor process MAY expose one MCP server endpoint for the concrete
+`ProjectRoot` owned by that process.
+
+The endpoint MUST:
+
+- bind only to the local machine in the initial implementation;
+- be associated with exactly one active Editor project;
+- use ephemeral endpoint and session-authentication metadata stored outside
+  canonical project authoring files;
+- disappear when that Editor process exits; and
+- route requests to the live Editor authoring host rather than reopening and
+  rewriting the same project independently.
+
+`engine-mcp` remains the transport-agnostic tool-handler adapter. MCP protocol
+binding, endpoint startup/shutdown, discovery metadata, and request routing
+belong above `engine-mcp` in the Editor/application layer or a dedicated thin
+transport crate that the Editor owns. They MUST NOT move transport or process
+lifecycle dependencies into `engine-authoring`.
+
+The first transport implementation uses a loopback-only MCP endpoint with an
+ephemeral per-Editor session credential. The exact loopback port is not
+persisted in project data. Remote-network authoring is outside this ADR.
+
+When no Editor is open, headless automation uses CLI in the first parity
+implementation. A future headless write-capable MCP host requires an explicit
+project-writer ownership contract before it may edit project data. It MUST NOT
+silently become a second writer beside a live Editor.
+
+### 3. The live Editor authoring session is authoritative
+
+When MCP is attached to an Editor, loaded authoring documents and their session
+revisions are authoritative. MCP write tools MUST call the same GUI-free
+services and transaction semantics used by the Editor and MUST NOT directly
+replace Scene, Graph, UI, Prefab, Material, settings, or animation files behind
+the Editor session.
+
+Persistence still occurs through the owning document's normal save pipeline.
+An MCP mutation may make an Editor document dirty in exactly the same sense as
+an equivalent human edit. Saving is a separate application concern unless a
+tool explicitly represents the existing shared save operation.
+
+### 4. One mutation tool call is one authoring transaction
+
+The first MCP authoring contract does not expose an open transaction that spans
+multiple MCP calls. Meaningful edits use bulk command tools so one request can
+carry all commands that must commit or roll back atomically.
+
+A successful mutation call:
+
+- starts from one authoritative document revision;
+- applies its complete command sequence to a private transaction working copy;
+- returns structured diagnostics and semantic diff information;
+- commits only when blocking validation succeeds; and
+- creates one undo history entry when attached to an interactive Editor
+  session.
+
+Preview and apply operations MUST carry enough document revision or generation
+identity to reject stale apply attempts rather than silently committing against
+a different source state.
+
+This resolves the previous question of transaction identity across multiple MCP
+calls: there is no cross-call transaction identity in the initial contract.
+Bulk single-call transactions are the supported mutation boundary.
+
+### 5. Shared domain services own authoring behavior
+
+Every parity implementation starts at the GUI-free shared layer, not in MCP or
+CLI. A domain that already has an authoritative service reuses it. A domain
+whose editing logic still lives only in Editor orchestration first extracts or
+adds the smallest coherent authoring service or command/query API at the owner
+layer.
+
+The service owns validation, command application, diff generation, and domain
+rules. Adapters own only transport-shaped DTOs, argument/file selection, and
+error/result formatting.
+
+The first new shared service is the Scene authoring service because the Scene
+model, `AuthoringCommand`, `Transaction`, schema registry, and Editor command
+path already exist. Behavior Tree remains the reference example through
+`BehaviorTreeAuthoringService`.
+
+### 6. Capability parity is semantic, not widget-for-tool mirroring
+
+The project does not require one MCP tool for every Editor button. It requires
+that every semantic project-authoring capability exposed by the Editor be
+reachable through the same shared authoring meaning from MCP, unless an ADR
+explicitly scopes it out.
+
+Parity-required capabilities include persisted semantic operations over:
+
+- project description and schema discovery;
+- scenes, entities, hierarchy, components, and properties;
+- asset discovery and inspection;
+- prefabs;
+- domain-neutral graphs and graph domains;
+- UI documents;
+- materials;
+- project settings; and
+- animation sets and other persisted animation authoring documents.
+
+Parity does not require MCP equivalents for transient Editor presentation such
+as window placement, hover state, current selection, panel layout, OS dialogs,
+or pointer coordinates. Runtime control and frame observation remain the AI
+Agent Bridge concern when they are not authoring mutations.
+
+### 7. Initial MCP and CLI surface is implemented in capability slices
+
+Implementation proceeds in the following order.
+
+1. **MCP lifecycle foundation**
+   - start and stop the project-scoped loopback MCP endpoint with the Editor;
+   - publish ephemeral discovery/authentication metadata outside project data;
+   - route tool calls to the live project authoring host; and
+   - enforce permission and stale-revision checks at shared boundaries.
+2. **Scene, entity, and schema parity**
+   - add shared Scene query/apply services;
+   - expose `project.describe`, Scene inspection/validation/preview/apply,
+     entity find/inspect, and component-schema discovery through MCP; and
+   - add equivalent CLI operations where headless use is meaningful.
+3. **Asset and Prefab parity**
+   - expose asset search/inspection; and
+   - route prefab creation and instantiation through existing shared prefab and
+     Scene command semantics.
+4. **Generic Graph and UI parity**
+   - expose domain-neutral graph inspect/validate/preview/apply/layout tools;
+   - keep Behavior Tree compile/domain tools as domain-specific extensions; and
+   - expose `UiDocumentCommand`/`UiDocumentTransaction` capabilities through
+     shared UI services.
+5. **Remaining typed document parity**
+   - add transactional service boundaries for Material, Project Settings,
+     Animation Set, and other persisted typed documents before adding MCP
+     write tools for them.
+6. **Parity guardrails**
+   - add capability coverage declarations and adapter-equivalence tests so
+     future Editor-only semantic authoring additions are detected by CI.
+
+The exact public tool inventory may evolve as bulk-operation ergonomics are
+validated, but tool design MUST preserve the shared service and transaction
+boundaries above.
+
+### 8. Adapter equivalence is a required test contract
+
+For authoring capabilities exposed through more than one adapter, tests MUST
+prove behavior equivalence at the shared semantic boundary. Given the same
+fixture and intent, Editor session orchestration, MCP, and CLI paths must not
+produce divergent business rules.
+
+Tests SHOULD compare the applicable combination of:
+
+- emitted command or command-sequence meaning;
+- validation diagnostics and stable diagnostic codes;
+- semantic preview diff;
+- committed document state;
+- deterministic serialized result when persistence is part of the operation;
+  and
+- stale-revision rejection.
+
+A shared capability identifier or registry SHOULD be owned by the authoring
+service layer so adapter coverage tests do not become an independently
+maintained list of Editor menu items.
+
+### 9. Permissions and path safety remain shared concerns
+
+The permission model from the authoring specification applies before mutation
+is committed. Read-only, preview-only, project-data write, asset write, code
+write, and command-execution capabilities MUST NOT be enforced only by MCP UI
+or transport code.
+
+All project paths continue to use the existing `ProjectRoot` confinement and
+canonical save/import rules. The loopback transport does not grant permission
+to escape the project root, overwrite arbitrary filesystem locations, or bypass
+approval requirements for destructive or elevated operations.
+
+## Consequences
+
+AI authoring becomes a first-class semantic client of the same model used by
+the human Editor instead of a screenshot-and-click automation layer. The active
+Editor remains the single in-memory authority for its project, so MCP edits can
+participate in normal dirty-state, validation, diff, and undo behavior without
+creating a competing file writer.
+
+Bulk single-call transactions keep MCP stateless enough to recover cleanly from
+client disconnects and avoid a server-side transaction registry. The tradeoff
+is that clients must group atomic edits before calling apply; large workflows
+may use several committed transactions rather than holding one transaction open
+across a conversation.
+
+The Editor gains local MCP endpoint lifecycle and authentication
+responsibilities. Headless MCP writing is intentionally not part of the first
+implementation, so CLI remains the supported headless mutation path until a
+future project-writer ownership decision permits another host safely.
+
+Completing parity is a multi-PR program rather than one adapter patch. Some
+domains, such as Material and Project Settings, require a proper transactional
+shared service before their MCP write surface can be considered complete.
+
+## Alternatives Considered
+
+### Use the AI Agent Bridge as the primary authoring interface
+
+Rejected. Mouse coordinates, key events, and screenshots are valuable for
+runtime and visual interaction but do not provide typed schemas, stable IDs,
+semantic diffs, deterministic validation, or command equivalence.
+
+### Make CLI the primary AI interface
+
+Rejected. CLI remains useful for headless and scripted operation, but MCP
+provides typed tool discovery and structured request/response semantics suited
+to interactive AI agents. Both adapters should converge on the same authoring
+services rather than choosing separate business logic.
+
+### Make MCP tools call CLI commands
+
+Rejected for the same reason as ADR 0015. Routing structured authoring through
+argv, stdout, and temporary files would add serialization layers and duplicate
+error mapping instead of sharing authoring services directly.
+
+### Run an independent write-capable MCP process against project files while the Editor is open
+
+Rejected. The Editor may contain dirty authoritative document state. A second
+writer could overwrite, lose, or conflict with unsaved human or AI edits. The
+initial MCP lifecycle therefore routes through the active Editor session.
+
+### Keep transactions open across multiple MCP calls
+
+Rejected for the initial contract. It would require server-side transaction
+identity, expiry, ownership, disconnect recovery, conflict policy, and undo
+semantics. Bulk command application already expresses the required atomicity
+without that lifecycle complexity.
+
+### Add MCP-specific mutation APIs for domains that lack shared commands
+
+Rejected. That would create the exact parity drift this ADR is intended to
+remove. The correct sequence is shared domain service first, then thin Editor,
+MCP, and CLI adapters.
+
+## Compatibility and Migration
+
+This ADR and its accompanying specification updates do not change persisted
+project data, stable IDs, serialized schemas, existing CLI output, or existing
+MCP tool names.
+
+Implementation will add MCP tools and internal authoring service APIs
+incrementally. Existing Behavior Tree MCP tools remain valid domain-specific
+operations. ADR 0035 AI Agent Bridge actions remain valid for input and frame
+observation.
+
+The new project-scoped MCP endpoint publishes only ephemeral application state
+outside the canonical project tree. No endpoint address, credential, or MCP
+session identifier may be written into `project.json`, project settings, asset
+manifests, Scene files, Graph files, or packaged game data.
